A firewall is a security system that monitors and controls network traffic entering or leaving a computer or network.

It uses predefined rules to decide whether traffic should be allowed or blocked.

Example:

Port 22 (SSH) → Allow
Port 23 (Telnet) → Block
Port 80 (HTTP) → Allow

In our practical, we used iptables to create a rule that blocks incoming TCP traffic on port 23:

sudo iptables -A INPUT -p tcp --dport 23 -j DROP

In one line:

A firewall protects a system by allowing trusted network traffic and blocking unwanted or unauthorized traffic.
